    "image_with_text_63xW3b": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text_iYVVL8": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<h2>Gathering Nectar</h2><p>Bees use their straw-like tongues to extract nectar from flowers, storing it in a specialized “honey stomach” for transport back to the hive.</p><h2>Transforming & Dehydrating</h2><p>Inside the hive, bees add enzymes to break down the nectar’s sugars, then deposit it into honeycomb cells. They fan their wings to evaporate moisture, transforming the nectar into thick, concentrated honey before sealing the cells with wax.</p><h2>Harvesting & Jarring</h2><p>Beekeepers remove the wax caps and spin the frames in an extractor. The honey is then filtered and bottled, preserving nature’s golden sweetness in every jar.</p>",
            "text_style": "body"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "text_iYVVL8"
      ],
